{
  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Philadelphia Phillies opened their season with an unmistakable jolt of energy. Brandon Marsh delivered a two-run single on Opening Day, and just days later Edmundo Sosa's clutch hit drove in two more in a sign of things to come. The offense was lively and the lineup deep, with contributions spreading across the roster in the early weeks of the season.\n\nApril 30th became the signature day of the Phillies' early surge, a single game that captured everything clicking at once — Schwarber's gap-splitting double, Stott's ringing triple, and Crawford's walk-off single capping a rally that felt like a statement. May only amplified the buzz, with Crawford hitting his first career home run and Stott launching a two-run blast on May 18th that swung a game Philadelphia desperately needed. Edmundo Sosa's two-run shot on May 30th carried that momentum into June, where the offense erupted in a wild back-to-back sequence on June 23rd and 24th — Realmuto, Marsh, and Stott all going yard before Derek Hill's walk-off blast the very next night gave the Phillies one of their most electric 48-hour stretches of the summer.\n\nThe dog days of late July and August brought turbulence. A gut-punch double play killed a key rally in July, and August was riddled with bullpen failures — Kerkering, Alvarado, and Shugart all surrendering critical runs in tight spots. Losses piled up and the wild card margin thinned. But Philadelphia refused to fold. Derek Hill's grand slam in early August steadied the ship, Edmundo Sosa's grand slam on August 28th was the single most valuable moment of the entire regular season, and late September clutch hits from Stott kept the Phillies alive just long enough to squeeze into the postseason.\n\nThe Wild Card round against Atlanta was a white-knuckle affair. Realmuto's go-ahead triple and early Bohm heroics gave the Phillies the lead, but Austin Riley's three-run blast off Jhoan Duran — the most damaging single play of the entire year — threw everything into chaos. The Phillies battled through the turbulence, ultimately surviving and advancing in a series that tested every nerve in Citizens Bank Park. The 88-win team, flawed and scrappy, had done enough to move on.",
